<tr style="max-width:100%">
<td style="text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:12px;color:#FFFFFF;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;padding-left:20px;margin:0;max-width:100%" valign="middle">DRIVING THE DAY</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</td>
</tr>
<tr style="max-width:100%">
<td style="padding:0 20px 0 20px;text-align:left;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:16px;color:#000000;max-width:100%"><p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;"><span style="color:#b70000;font-weight:bold;font-family:sans-serif;"><strong style="font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-weight:bold;">THE MOMENT OF TRUTH: </strong></span>The House will vote on the farm bill Thursday, as <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.zmXQYxftKFWCpJXVm9O1dggo6mIaXIcCdz5Oy3GFGIg8XF5807nFipGERy7PWlyhOAGGl08APKVbfKbu8cvEsMdxDYjtrv5ue8i_eFgaUUQ/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h4/h001.R6ZXtfcKx3nLqlhr-yX7Dd4GJuYDoEIDRn0lNDQPLa4" target="_blank">Meredith reported</a> over the weekend.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">The legislation faces a narrow path forward as some Republicans, including Rep. <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.2Z4DCAMF7Pq6fUenFyNGNTJibOcoTusa12Jh7uHsl_ykQ4p_ZxUtLl9VvXxUjv0xDJorxZcu8J5V9ydIrPntJqAwU6Oh_QV3flaZ779v4iZuALEq1vzf3OpxocUKF_cA/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h5/h001.HVP94H053ZJCArda3k5ciM8Iz55B3GTZV2J6QYi5BS4" target="_blank">Anna Paulina Luna</a> (R-Fla.), threaten to oppose it unless its pesticide labeling language is removed. With the GOP’s slim majority, even a small cadre of Republicans could block the bill’s passage as most Democrats also plan to vote against it.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">The provision, which would prevent pesticide makers from being sued over cancer warning labeling, is the biggest source of conflict surrounding the bill, <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.6g0Zd3AyneOViJYBXgbV65ZWHEH5gWGSDKLrcKzwr38gR5U-7DNKZ_ZS7HCZH1LVMJt8nKSRIxBUUIHU9IahjyDs-mOUKhjFzGDixH6NN9rS4b_t0pfq_fLET2LGpigvbx_m6vX_1TWzMRGmDMV1uw/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h6/h001.LxDL8Bk3ZwDGZlsoZaq4aEYjwxuDw7z_fZGiI0_ANtk" target="_blank">as Grace and Meredith reported</a>. However, other potential “yes” voters are also threatening to vote “no” over myriad issues.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">President Donald Trump and many Republican lawmakers have said they feel urgency to pass the bill, which could sway rural voters in November’s midterm elections, especially as farm country struggles with the high costs of fertilizer and fuel stemming from the war in Iran.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">Other big questions about the legislation’s final form loom ahead of Thursday, and the House Rules Committee will meet at 1 p.m. today to consider hundreds of amendments on issues including pesticides, food aid and the blocking of California’s Proposition 12.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">Rep. <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.2Z4DCAMF7Pq6fUenFyNGNTJibOcoTusa12Jh7uHsl_ykQ4p_ZxUtLl9VvXxUjv0xiekW8B9Sb6wMzVj8sG2sdozQ3D0OaOH9uO7AC0yxNFqRwofeHzyle0l0n4OkgrmX/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h7/h001.GOsrkpQS16vdUnS93JxNYy5Hx_-sd0THwOqS-BilEbc" target="_blank">Andrew Garbarino</a> (R-N.Y.) told MA Sunday that his top request is a vote on an amendment to remove the “Save Our Bacon Act,” which would overturn Proposition 12, from the bill. He said he hasn’t gotten an indication of whether the Rules Committee or leaders will push for a vote on it.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">He said he won’t oppose a procedural vote on the rule but that his vote on final passage will depend on how the bill changes during the Rules process.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">“I know some of my colleagues are not happy with my stance — one of them told me I was breaking his heart,” Garbarino said, adding that he was concerned about how the bill would affect existing rules and regulations in New York.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">“So I think we have to be very careful about the way all this all moves forward,” he said.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">A bipartisan <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.dgGKIUEMNqic94f5rSpPKHVjx9T1ykg4VCn1RopSOIfTTcRFjMJVePcVDjHQalPhOSZV1EV8gFVfH03prQioVoPB1CC-v6sQ6N-YanelMuqXiV5DUC2hwWTXAbf3TAYKKvHp7wg_2AypgvjbCYeiTQpyKvf5Oi0sJ7oQK1ph7QMMISYTOEtkxSfjzhWhxCS_laQKw__dyKATKgLCPVNngSg1i8eMecTGNdVtWSScdoV6fXtzVi34uQ-5Xl1VMctm4tTjPBu8-ZCLfscI9Hvu6g/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h8/h001.YYQfFSMe6t9XT0HMXn_He7yHiBZSwW6auGt48S6ruzo" target="_blank">amendment</a> introduced by Rep. <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.2Z4DCAMF7Pq6fUenFyNGNTJibOcoTusa12Jh7uHsl_ykQ4p_ZxUtLl9VvXxUjv0x4qtg_eZ-H-U-g6pNmH5RtZDTH2U1sQsJqZiK26rakwlFlM6EN7vtEyiaGoTAyetX/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h9/h001.dj3z7Wve_bUfxvCd6hrnzSXCpBTBCjHAlMQtUeOywjc" target="_blank">Michelle Fischbach</a> (R-Minn.) would allow the year-round sale of E15 and reform the Renewable Fuel Standard. The proposal had 20 co-sponsors, including House Rural Domestic Energy Council Co-Chairs <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.2Z4DCAMF7Pq6fUenFyNGNTJibOcoTusa12Jh7uHsl_ykQ4p_ZxUtLl9VvXxUjv0xaAI5KoTVa536bvhNxvY1zq-Tf-JmeWNdrCr8ftP4Mi48ufk2L8U7XIhEN5kF8p08/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h10/h001.PWxXJLfW12xTBf6sgFmv-dx5fhMxv27pxiFDhlWi6XM" target="_blank">Randy Feenstra</a> (R-Iowa) and <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.2Z4DCAMF7Pq6fUenFyNGNTJibOcoTusa12Jh7uHsl_ykQ4p_ZxUtLl9VvXxUjv0xfSxeB_J-jtFov1yW4tuz2pv5zoCNONTkXjAy12xJbE8rU_7qTruWRhSQ16RolTzM/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h11/h001.iCW_ZcEa8Pb5nkFqNfo684CMml7lmgXf3_WHaxWy8LQ" target="_blank">Stephanie Bice</a> (R-Okla.).</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">While year-round E15 enjoys widespread bipartisan support among ag-state lawmakers, Ag Committee leaders have said the issue is not in its jurisdiction, so it would require a waiver from House leadership to be lumped into the farm bill. It is unclear whether Speaker <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.2Z4DCAMF7Pq6fUenFyNGNTJibOcoTusa12Jh7uHsl_ykQ4p_ZxUtLl9VvXxUjv0xCQdQh3zN2gupDn0l9yFDuDK2K6YQ8ZdAaOFwJ2G4viATQIQBeWIs5Q8fc-d8XHBI/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h12/h001.whf1rg_k4uWzt-qCg83NgiSSgEK5J6lCSoD5Fh3Vx38" target="_blank">Mike Johnson</a> will grant the waiver.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;"><strong style="font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-weight:bold;">Where the Dems stand: </strong>Two of the seven Democrats who voted to advance the farm bill out of committee, Reps. <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.2Z4DCAMF7Pq6fUenFyNGNTJibOcoTusa12Jh7uHsl_ykQ4p_ZxUtLl9VvXxUjv0xn783DseKNyqX4guxGwkcYPXrGt8fQJFmiJDxhsDyvOO2UG-zue6naEQ3z066sxk1/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h13/h001.TD1W84ZD4R_ffJUbslclPGtzfmV5yviWzKeyGo2WDQw" target="_blank">Josh Riley</a> (D-N.Y.) and <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.2Z4DCAMF7Pq6fUenFyNGNTJibOcoTusa12Jh7uHsl_ykQ4p_ZxUtLl9VvXxUjv0xOSM-l4Fvz5vuwPaYBQpqxnfP2J1_YBINXP_3pIq8vqeWufyDU5aA35SRX_hKR33g/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h14/h001.Xq1Xz0AfAUX-IE3RKrVEs-6Qvk6lKLghZ-5RyQ_nR24" target="_blank">Kristen McDonald Rivet</a> (D-Mich.) <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.lWesBdxv006jasBZWiiGk9P_ybPe2CGyS-6CLrg5BxYWkXV4TejjmX9mNkoHKTH4xH6vF4DhKuev2NC3Si7tFXkrp83oA-FJdKKRE13_i-COBV4LHUZ_qfOfVRCImyO-l7Crg0cdGo3osrCkra9A7OPF77DBbNml3JRVWaOMw9U/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h15/h001.V0fyKPW7JLBF__7nQ_KkWfHyXvKFhlIngseFe1Z3zhc" target="_blank">told MA</a> that they plan to vote for it again on the floor. But Republicans will likely still have to count on centrist Democrats voting for the bill to have a chance at passage due to infighting over the contentious provisions.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;"><strong style="font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-weight:bold;">Also on the Hill this week:</strong> The House Appropriations Committee will mark up the Ag-FDA budget for fiscal 2027 Tuesday. It is expected to run late into the night and could spill into Wednesday.<br/></p></td>
</tr>
