<td style="padding:0 20px 0 20px;text-align:left;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:16px;color:#000000;max-width:100%"><p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;"><span style="color:#b70000;font-weight:bold;font-family:sans-serif;"><strong style="font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-weight:bold;">FIRST IN MA:</strong> </span>The Department of Agriculture and one of its staff unions will begin facing off today over the department’s planned day care closures.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">The USDA Child Development Center will cease operations this summer after the company running the facility declined to sign a new contract with the department, as <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.lWesBdxv006jasBZWiiGk9P_ybPe2CGyS-6CLrg5BxZGfZS3TDSTqdEhMWY4IFGQuyMPv0EbMLsth956LftpyhLdTKyCQ5yV0rIk2dVow8D8a1HHpWQ5RFktiaP-VquJ/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h22/h001.VpIIclGJAnljLfm763FEeQuc3Uo7zUesDjn5Xq4S09c" target="_blank">your host first reported last week</a>.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">But Collin Bradley, president of AFSCME Local 3976, which represents staff at the Foreign Agricultural Service, said the union did not receive notice of the closures from the USDA itself, as required by its bargaining agreement.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">KinderCare notified USDA parents that the day cares in the department’s Yates Building and the George Washington Carver Center would close on July 10, according to two KinderCare emails obtained by POLITICO.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">“I think one of the things that has angered people so much is the complete lack of communication or care from the department level,” Bradley told MA. “It seems like they're not even telling their subagencies about it and they're just leaving employees in a lurch.”</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">Bradley said USDA has also not fulfilled <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.6g0Zd3AyneOViJYBXgbV63aXJJ0o4RIuVjkPQBonL393PVPE3U7wMEQOGJJkwjut7hP0RttYbfFJKVA9pHxzeT0rkF2-ZDBK2eQijf9WV6pW1mfhigmZ2inxkS0F83WO/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h23/h001.00i-SD3F8xEy2Ry9K5EoddDK4JxmeiD5Y0Xb0pFf5M4" target="_blank">a union contract</a> child care provision related to the department’s sweeping reorganization plans.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">“We have a specific language about if they move employees away from the South building, they have to do a survey of the employees, see what the child care needs are and then make changes based on those recommendations,” Bradley said. “That has not been done yet, even though it's been a year since they announced that they're moving people out.”</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">USDA <a href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.6g0Zd3AyneOViJYBXgbV65ZWHEH5gWGSDKLrcKzwr38H3_qFVMuPrLz6osvdJjsg9Ec4E0WNwPCG8XyENXbMcfR0DoJKswpwiffQnOeXeeXsselYArLrAAO0D8hQukymWd790Omlr7Z3dui7z2zERpB_7yw_ZBL_yMxAogXAuDI/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h24/h001.gsdZxLhiSa-g-e_zYoVVNiD_1HncEDEGqJGpakukkP0" target="_blank">announced in July 2025</a> that it would relocate much of its Washington-area staff to regional hubs across the country. Deputy Agriculture Secretary Stephen Vaden has said he expects employees will move to their <a data-debug-source-site="POLITICO Pro" data-debug-source-uuid="0000019b-f74f-d452-a99b-fffffeda0000" href="https://url4027.email.politico.com/ss/c/u001.6g0Zd3AyneOViJYBXgbV65ZWHEH5gWGSDKLrcKzwr3-AN9UjD61gICYBRvMhOIKTTdKEj2_6Ryl8wKcY2ZKIYBDPvieAwHNwa7eX8OEq_UBARaQ2FOBdowm2GLG3fP5nGecTLVdPfv98C4N5tNmoOr68tioP2RjFvHaUMpw2DFQ/4q5/NlalU5gmRhW8spN89i56Xw/h25/h001.dtcX4tJTFuNZQ35r9YqZF8bPDSRh7zUfYmLsNarRMKY" target="_blank">reassigned locations this summer</a>, coinciding with the day cares’ closure.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">USDA spokesperson Alec Varsamis said in a statement that “the impending closure of the KinderCare facility in the Yates Building has nothing to do with the Department’s reorganization; as has been made clear, the Yates building will remain and continue to house USDA employees.”</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">Some USDA employees who use the day cares said the closures will mean added strain to their lives, longer commutes and possibly higher costs, as department employees pay a subsidized lower fee to enroll in the in-house child care.</p>
<p style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:24px;line-height:24px;">Union representatives are scheduled to meet with USDA’s labor relations staff Monday afternoon to begin a bargaining process over the planned closure. Bradley said that because child care is an essential service, it is incumbent upon USDA to find another day care vendor for the space.<br/></p></td>
</tr>
